  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nMorning showers and thunderstorms have made for a tricky \nforecast as to how afternoon/evening convection will unfold. \nOutflow from this morning's storms which has become the \neffective boundary has pushed south of the area. This will be \nwhere the best chance for more organized severe storms will be \nhowever, the actual surface front is currently bisecting the \narea and may be the focus for additional storm development this \nafternoon/evening. Hi-res guidance suggests storm development \nmay be forced by a mid-level shortwave moving through the area \non zonal flow aloft. However, model guidance does differ on \ntiming and intensity of this shortwave feature. Storms are \nexpect to develop in an area of 1500-2000J/Kg of MUCAPE and \n40-45kts of effective shear. This will be sufficient for strong\nto isolated severe storms capable of producing large hail up to\nping pong ball size and gusty to damaging winds to 60mph. Storm\ncoverage is expected to increase in coverage and perhaps \nintensity (after a brief diurnal weakening trend) around \nmidnight as a nocturnal 35-40kt southwesterly LLJ noses into \nthe area. These storms will be very efficient with PWAT values \naround 2 inches so, if even brief training occurs localized \nflooding would be a concern. Storms will persist through the \novernight into Sunday morning until the LLJ weakens and veers \noff to the east. At this time Sunday afternoon looks mostly dry \nalthough a few scattered showers (20-30%) may linger across the \neastern CWA. Highs Sunday afternoon will range from the lower \n70s north to mid 80s south. By tomorrow night, the surface front\nthat moved through the area today will reside south of the CWA.\nAgain a nocturnal LLJ will develop and override this boundary \nproducing additional showers and thunderstorms across the area. \nSevere potential looks low at this time however, heavy rain may \nagain be possible with PWATs remaining around 2.00 inches. This \nmay lead to additional flooding concerns. Showers may linger \ninto Monday morning as the frontal boundary lifts north across \nthe area as a warm front. This will set up the area for a very \nwarm day on Monday as strong WAA ushers in temperatures rising \ninto the mid 80s to mid 90s with heat index values over the \nsouthern CWA rising to 100-105.\n\nMonday night into Tuesday, a upper level trough will move from the \nnorthern Plains into the northern Great Lakes. This will force a \nweak trailing cold front into the area on Tuesday. The local area \nwill be on the periphery of a upper level ridge over the \nsoutheastern CONUS. Models through the middle of next week bring \nunsettled weather to the area...depicting several shortwaves \nrounding the upper level ridge and bringing rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms to the area with the stalled surface front over the \narea being the focus for storm activity. while severe potential \nlooks low at this time, several rounds of storms are expected to \nproduce 2-4 inches of rain across northern Missouri which may lead \nto flooding concerns. While the stagnant upper level pattern does \nbreak down late next week it does come courtesy of an upper level \ntrough moving from the central Plains and through the local area \nwhich would keep the pattern unsettled.",
